{"agent":"lane-rec-surface","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The module and the acceptance criteria state that every refusal names the nearest verb, but several validation refusals do not. For example, with `[records].correct_reason_max_chars` set to its configured limit, `ddflow correct TASK-1 --field body --text x --reason <reason longer than the limit>` returns the failure from `_check_request` ending with “Say why it was wrong, not what it said.” and never includes `NEAREST` (`ddflow correct <id> --field NAME --text TEXT --reason WHY`). The same omission occurs when `len(fields) > rc.correct_max_fields`, so the CLI/MCP user is not told the nearest verb for those refusals.","digest":"5ba8e8bfc7be8128","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/api/record_correct.py:_check_request","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/record_correct.py:_check_request"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"`_author` does not respect the record kind selected by `_resolve_kind`, even though `_resolve_kind` explicitly supports the same `rid` existing as more than one correctable kind. `_author` accepts any event whose prefix is in `(kind, \"task\", \"phase\", \"item\")`. Given a subject `X` that is both a task and a phase, if agent A created the task event for `X` first and agent B created the phase event for `X` later, then `ddflow correct X --kind phase ...` from agent B resolves the kind to `phase`, but `_author` can return agent A from the earlier task event. Agent B is then treated as not the author and may be refused despite correcting its own phase record; conversely agent A may be allowed to correct agent B’s phase record as the “author.” This violates the D-correction-permissions-2 rule that an agent may correct its own record without asking.","digest":"f2af767694b70202","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/api/record_correct.py:_author","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/record_correct.py:_author"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"Adding `\"correct\"` to the help-topic list creates a top-level help topic, but the manifest contains no `ddflow/templates/prompts/help/correct.md` and the only new help prose is added under `ddflow/templates/prompts/help/update.md`. Given `ddflow help correct`, the help service will look for a `correct` topic/template that was not added and will either fail with a missing-topic/file error or show no help, contradicting the stated CLI help-topic requirement.","digest":"f57460a01745e27c","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/help.py:91","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/help.py:91"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`_text_of` only catches `OSError` when reading `--from-file`, but `Path.read_text()` can also raise `UnicodeDecodeError` (a `ValueError`) when the file contains bytes invalid in the current encoding. For example, `ddflow correct BUG-1 --field body --from-file bad.bin --reason \"wrong\"` with a non-text/binary file will escape this helper as an unhandled exception instead of returning the intended `cannot read ...` failure.","digest":"73b23f3333c63271","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/record_correct.py:26","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/surfaces/declared/record_correct.py:26"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The new help paragraph claims CLI/MCP parity from one declaration, but the documented MCP action list omits `from_file` while the CLI examples immediately above use `--from-file`. An MCP caller following the documented parameters (`id`, `field`, `text`, `reason`, `kind`, `secret`, `approval`) has no way to supply the file-backed input path that the CLI surface supports. If the declared `correct` action actually exposes `from_file`, this help text is wrong and will fail the help/README parity acceptance; if it does not, then the declared command is not the CLI/MCP parity promised by the intent.","digest":"8d9bc3c7359bf4f2","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/update.md:35","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/templates/prompts/help/update.md:35"},{"chunk":7,"detail":"The second part of this test calls the MCP alias `ddflow_correct` and only asserts `not bad`; it never asserts that the alias actually changed the record or emitted a second `record.corrected` event. If the alias is registered in `TOOLS`/`F.ALIASES` but its handler is a no-op or updates the wrong record while returning a successful MCP result, the test still passes, so it does not establish CLI/MCP parity for the alias. Given a `ddflow_correct` alias that returns `{\"ok\": true}` without modifying `B1`, the assertions after the first `ddflow_task` call still pass and the alias call’s `not bad` check still passes, leaving the broken alias untested.","digest":"f5c7daf1b76a7491","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_record_correct_surface.py:test_cli_and_mcp_are_one_declaration","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_record_correct_surface.py:test_cli_and_mcp_are_one_declaration"}],"chunks_total":7,"coverage":"7/7 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":41986,"diff_sha":"d57d0bfa723da98af2f890e56d1077c3c13d2a62670130be8daa8a4228f06504","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/REC-correct-surface","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":619.7,"excluded_bytes":9925,"excluded_files":2,"excluded_paths":["README.md","tests/golden/json/result_schemas.json"],"family":"alibaba","findings":6,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"[REDACTED:name]","output_digest":"42f0efb94c87be5f","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/REC-correct-surface.rubber_duck.[REDACTED:name].20261112T222127Z-fa0e8743.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7],"reviewed_head